1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,760
They're slick operators in liquid, semi-liquid or solid form.

2
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:11,680
The biggest machines and the smallest depend on lubricants.

3
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:17,980
Without them, the world's gears, wheels and bearings would grind to a halt.

4
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:21,940
Now, Lube Job on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,040
The maintenance schedule says it's time for a lube job.

6
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:46,600
But instead of heading to a gas station, we've come to a bigger facility.

7
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:51,220
That's because the vehicle to be serviced is somewhat bigger than a car.

8
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:56,280
And it's more complex, so the lube job is going to take a little longer.

9
00:00:56,281 --> 00:01:00,600
This is a Boeing 757.

10
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:04,920
We do it every six months or so. We'll have the airplane completely lubed.

11
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:08,900
We have to lube about 700 lube points on the aircraft or lube areas.

12
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:14,540
And this will be done accomplished in approximately 50 man hours.

13
00:01:16,380 --> 00:01:20,120
Welcome to the American Airlines maintenance facility in Tulsa, Oklahoma,

14
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:23,840
where the lubing begins with the 757's wings.

15
00:01:23,841 --> 00:01:29,440
On the wing of this airplane, we have to lubricate all the flight control surfaces to change the shape of the wings.

16
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:39,020
These surfaces include the spoilers, the leading edge slats, the ailerons, and the flaps.

17
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:45,080
All the pivot points, actuators, and associated mechanisms must be greased.

18
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:48,600
The lubricants we use on these aircraft have to face extreme challenges.

19
00:01:48,601 --> 00:01:54,980
They have to be able to work and operate and be useful in minus 60 degrees when they're flying up at altitude,

20
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:57,440
down to sitting on the ramp at 130 degrees.

21
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:05,800
The synthetic grease for the flight control surfaces can withstand temperatures as frigid as minus 100 degrees Fahrenheit,

22
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:09,180
or as searing as plus 250.

23
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:12,540
We're on the tail of the aircraft now.

24
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:15,160
This is the elevator that controls the pitch of the aircraft,

25
00:02:15,161 --> 00:02:20,540
And the gentlemen behind me are lubeing the elevator hinge points and also the action

26
00:02:20,541 --> 00:02:22,920
requirements.

27
00:02:22,921 --> 00:02:26,340
Workers also have to lube the rudder.

28
00:02:26,341 --> 00:02:29,600
Meanwhile the rest of the team has moved to the landing gear.

29
00:02:29,601 --> 00:02:33,420
At the lower points, which collect intense heat from the brakes, the technicians switch

30
00:02:33,421 --> 00:02:42,800
to a special high temperature grease, which can withstand up to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.

31
00:02:42,801 --> 00:02:47,040
Finally, it's time to lube the massive turbofan engines.

32
00:02:47,041 --> 00:02:55,720
The engine on our Boeing 757 aircraft is in the 40,000-pound thrust range, and the engine

33
00:02:55,721 --> 00:03:00,880
oil quantity is only about 20 quarts.

34
00:03:00,881 --> 00:03:04,740
Airline engineers are so confident about modern synthetic turbine oils that in this

35
00:03:04,741 --> 00:03:07,980
jet, the oil is rarely changed.

36
00:03:07,981 --> 00:03:09,560
It's usually just topped off.

37
00:03:09,561 --> 00:03:21,577
Through the normal replenishment that occurs as they use oil, we find that the oil stays good enough, fresh enough to operate very satisfactorily without draining and

38
00:03:21,578 --> 00:03:22,880
refilling the oil.

39
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:29,380
It's taken four technicians one day, but at last this lube job is done.

40
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:32,220
One plane, 700 lube points.

41
00:03:34,620 --> 00:03:39,240
Elsewhere in our machine-driven world, there are countless mechanical points to be lubricated.

42
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:53,560
And there are a vast number of lubricants to do the job.

43
00:03:55,740 --> 00:04:00,920
The main function of a lubricant is to reduce friction and wear between two rubbing surfaces.

44
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:06,080
Lubricants do this by forming a film, which separates those surfaces.

45
00:04:06,081 --> 00:04:10,700
Take away that film, and the result can lead to mechanical failure.

46
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:14,300
If it weren't for lubricants, things would wear out a lot faster,

47
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:18,760
and we'd have a lot higher energy and power losses than we currently experience.

48
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:23,580
Wind turbines put lubricants to a particularly severe test.

49
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:28,560
Some must endure over a trillion cycles on their rotating elements over 20 years' spans.

50
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:32,560
Under severe circumstances, if those oils weren't doing a good job

51
00:04:32,561 --> 00:04:34,860
or if the gearbox had some sort of a failure,

52
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:37,920
then the rotating propeller wouldn't be able to turn the gearbox,

53
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:39,500
so you wouldn't generate electricity,

54
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:42,700
but you'd generate an awful lot of heat, damage the gearbox,

55
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:44,620
and potentially even have a fire.

56
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:50,200
Only proper lubrication in advance can prevent such a scenario.

57
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:56,420
But the turbine's 300-foot height makes a lube job a towering chore.

58
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:00,780
Once applied, the lubricants must survive extreme pressures

59
00:05:00,781 --> 00:05:03,180
produced by the 75,000-pound rotor,

60
00:05:03,740 --> 00:05:06,300
and they also have to stand up to the elements.

61
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:09,900
They're typically deployed in very harsh environments,

62
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:11,560
very remote locations,

63
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:15,460
usually exposed to very harsh moisture conditions

64
00:05:15,461 --> 00:05:16,800
as well as extremes in temperature,

65
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:18,900
also wind-driven dust.

66
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:23,080
A wind turbine typically will operate for 20 to 22 hours a day,

67
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:24,320
unlike an automobile,

68
00:05:24,321 --> 00:05:26,380
which you may only drive one to two hours a day.

69
00:05:26,381 --> 00:05:33,760
The lubricants must take this punishment over a very long haul.

70
00:05:33,761 --> 00:05:40,660
The gearbox oil of wind turbines in remote locations may not get changed for seven years.

71
00:05:40,661 --> 00:05:46,300
If the oil breaks down within the wind turbine gearbox and we do not have adequate lubrication,

72
00:05:46,301 --> 00:05:49,280
we will have premature wear of the gearbox.

73
00:05:49,281 --> 00:05:55,480
To prevent such breakdowns, chemists have concocted a synthetic oil the technicians

74
00:05:55,481 --> 00:06:01,480
pump into the gearbox at the factory. The lubricant can weather extreme temperatures

75
00:06:01,481 --> 00:06:02,907
and last for years.

76
00:06:02,908 --> 00:06:08,240
Being much thicker than, say, a car's oil, allows it to withstand heavy

77
00:06:08,241 --> 00:06:09,774
mechanical loads.

78
00:06:09,775 --> 00:06:16,720
And to reduce gearbox wear, the oil contains particles of the solid lubricant

79
00:06:16,721 --> 00:06:18,980
molybdenum disulfide.

80
00:06:18,981 --> 00:06:24,260
We do a lot to make sure that the oil inside this gearbox is maintained at adequate temperature,

81
00:06:24,261 --> 00:06:26,240
proper temperature and proper cleanliness.

82
00:06:26,241 --> 00:06:30,000
Therefore, we have a system on the gearbox for cooling the oil,

83
00:06:30,001 --> 00:06:33,540
and we also have a system on the gearbox for heating the oil.

84
00:06:33,541 --> 00:06:35,600
This is a filtration system for the gearbox,

85
00:06:35,601 --> 00:06:38,080
very critical to deliver clean, cool oil

86
00:06:38,081 --> 00:06:39,540
to all the bearings in the gearbox.

87
00:06:45,080 --> 00:06:47,580
The origins of lubricants date back a lot farther

88
00:06:47,581 --> 00:06:49,720
than your last oil change.

89
00:06:49,721 --> 00:06:52,280
Evidence of one of the earliest lubricant discoveries

90
00:06:52,281 --> 00:06:57,320
was from early Mesopotamia, dating to about 3250 BC.

91
00:06:57,321 --> 00:06:59,460
An early potter's wheel that was discovered

92
00:06:59,461 --> 00:07:02,660
showed evidence of hydrocarbon lubricants, probably

93
00:07:02,661 --> 00:07:07,120
from a tar seepage in the local area.

94
00:07:07,121 --> 00:07:08,900
Relics from Egyptian burial sites

95
00:07:08,901 --> 00:07:13,640
reveal that ancient wheels of other kinds were also lubed.

96
00:07:13,641 --> 00:07:17,880
Several chariots excavated from Egyptian tombs

97
00:07:17,881 --> 00:07:21,300
showed evidence of lubrication on the axles.

98
00:07:21,301 --> 00:07:24,840
This was oxidized animal fat, possibly from mutton or beef.

99
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:30,760
For the next three millennia, technology advanced, but lubricants did not.

100
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:38,540
For thousands of years, we had to rely on two different types of natural lubricants,

101
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:41,360
animal fats and vegetable oils.

102
00:07:41,520 --> 00:07:47,300
A good example would be olive oil and also beef tallow for animal fats.

103
00:07:47,301 --> 00:07:54,440
Even the engineering wonder of the Middle Ages, the mechanical clock, was probably lubricated with animal fats.

104
00:07:55,840 --> 00:07:59,860
In warm, arid climates, the lubricant of choice was olive oil.

105
00:08:00,580 --> 00:08:02,220
In China, soybean oil.

106
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:06,020
In northwestern Europe, lards and tallows.

107
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:14,820
The Industrial Revolution produced a string of new techno-wonders, but little that was new to lubricate them.

108
00:08:14,821 --> 00:08:22,120
The era, however, does provide the earliest written accounts of one of the first solid lubricants, graphite.

109
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:28,640
Workers ground the mineral into a slippery powder, which was used to maintain both small and large machinery in mills.

110
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:32,760
What makes graphite slippery?

111
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:42,100
It has a crystalline structure that's layered, with strong bonds between atoms within a layer, but weak bonds between atoms of different layers.

112
00:08:42,940 --> 00:08:45,280
This allows the layers to slide on one another.

113
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:52,480
But by and large, the groundbreaking machines of the Industrial Revolution were lubed with the same old stuff.

114
00:08:53,120 --> 00:08:54,880
Though, with a new twist.

115
00:08:55,680 --> 00:09:01,600
They were still using the same lubricants, goose grease, lard, palm oil, things like that.

116
00:09:01,601 --> 00:09:04,200
But they started mixing them in exotic combinations.

117
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:09,820
There was one organic lubricant of the era, however, that trumped the rest.

118
00:09:09,821 --> 00:09:13,100
a liquid hydrocarbon produced by the sperm whale.

119
00:09:14,980 --> 00:09:20,500
Sperm whale oil is a natural ester, and it stands up better to oxidation.

120
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:25,540
It doesn't turn rancid and smell bad, and on top of that, it's an excellent lubricant.

121
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:31,440
Sperm whale oil had unique properties in that it had a good shelf life.

122
00:09:31,441 --> 00:09:36,560
It had good high-temperature properties and didn't break down at elevated temperatures

123
00:09:36,561 --> 00:09:40,060
as readily as other vegetable and animal-based oils.

124
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:43,920
And so it became sort of the gold standard for lubricants.

125
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:49,860
The sperm whale was hunted for its oil from the 1700s until the early 1980s.

126
00:09:51,920 --> 00:09:55,840
The liquid was extracted from the blubber and huge head cavity of the whale,

127
00:09:56,180 --> 00:09:58,560
a unique feature compared to other whale species.

128
00:10:00,620 --> 00:10:05,400
Sperm whale oil was used for high-precision instruments such as clocks,

129
00:10:05,401 --> 00:10:10,080
high-speed machinery and other delicate equipment that needed lubrication.

130
00:10:11,260 --> 00:10:16,600
But during the Industrial Revolution, people used sperm whale oil primarily for illumination.

131
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:20,620
It was the fuel burned most widely in lamps.

132
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:27,700
And these beautiful creatures were almost hunted to extinction because of this use,

133
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:33,180
and it was becoming very expensive to secure sperm whale oil for these uses.

134
00:10:33,181 --> 00:10:38,240
So the search was on for another fuel to replace sperm whale oil.

135
00:10:39,660 --> 00:10:42,240
One possibility, petroleum.

136
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:48,360
For centuries, people living where it seeped naturally to the surface collected it for various uses.

137
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:54,920
Egyptians used petroleum for their advanced mummification techniques.

138
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:01,420
Some Native Americans sealed their canoes with it,

139
00:11:01,421 --> 00:11:05,740
while other Americans even drank it for their health.

140
00:11:07,580 --> 00:11:10,860
In the early 19th century and on,

141
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:16,200
there were many folks gathering up these oils and selling them as all-purpose cures

142
00:11:16,201 --> 00:11:21,880
to cure anything from jaundice to liver disease to the gout.

143
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:26,560
Petroleum had little significant application until 1849,

144
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:30,980
when innovators developed a process for distilling a lamp fuel from coal.

145
00:11:31,420 --> 00:11:34,440
A light oil called kerosene.

146
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:44,480
In the mid-1800s, they discovered that they could take coal and heat it up and cook the oils out of it.

147
00:11:45,020 --> 00:11:46,120
They got two cuts.

148
00:11:46,260 --> 00:11:50,280
One was kerosene and one was a thicker oil that was used for lubrication.

149
00:11:51,720 --> 00:11:56,880
Kerosene was easy to make, smelled better than sperm whale oil as it burned, and didn't spoil.

150
00:11:57,940 --> 00:11:59,700
It created less smoke.

151
00:11:59,701 --> 00:12:06,860
It provided a more even and bright light, and with the discovery of being able to just pump oil out of the ground

152
00:12:06,861 --> 00:12:10,060
and put a little bit of distillation on it to create lots of kerosene,

153
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:18,040
it drove the price of kerosene way down and really drove the use of whale oil out of the lamp oil market.

154
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:25,760
By 1857, kerosene lanterns had snuffed out whale oil lamps.

155
00:12:25,761 --> 00:12:33,220
But petroleum lubricants had not replaced the old animal fats in plant seed oils, which were cheaper to make.

156
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:41,320
However, a new method of producing petroleum was about to revolutionize lubrication and the world.

157
00:12:45,720 --> 00:12:55,220
The cavity in the head of a sperm whale, called the spermaceti organ, holds up to 530 gallons of oil and can extend through 40% of the whale's length.

158
00:12:55,760 --> 00:13:01,100
Lube Job will return on Modern Marvelous, here on the History Channel.

159
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:09,180
We now return to Lube Job on Modern Marvelous.

160
00:13:14,260 --> 00:13:19,200
When it comes to lubricating today's machines, petroleum products dominate.

161
00:13:19,201 --> 00:13:28,320
It's hard to imagine that just 150 years ago, most machinery was lubed with plant and animal-based compounds.

162
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:36,660
But in 1859, in the oil slick fields of Pennsylvania,

163
00:13:37,280 --> 00:13:41,960
an entrepreneur and adventurer named Colonel Edwin Drake would discover lubrication's future.

164
00:13:41,961 --> 00:13:48,540
Colonel Drake was able to hire a drilling team and secure the necessary supplies and the necessary land

165
00:13:48,541 --> 00:13:50,340
to drill a successful oil well.

166
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:52,460
And when he discovered oil,

167
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:55,420
it really changed the entire industry overnight

168
00:13:55,421 --> 00:13:58,700
because all of a sudden they had a ready supply

169
00:13:58,701 --> 00:14:02,460
of literally hundreds and hundreds and hundreds of gallons of oil a day

170
00:14:02,461 --> 00:14:03,740
coming out of the ground,

171
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:07,180
and that oil was immediately useful to lubricate.

172
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:13,040
Soon, lubricants made from crude oil replaced those from plants and animals.

173
00:14:14,900 --> 00:14:17,940
Not only were they cheaper, but they withstood heat better

174
00:14:17,941 --> 00:14:19,140
and didn't putrefy.

175
00:14:21,980 --> 00:14:25,940
The new lubricants greased the wheels of a vast array of new machines,

176
00:14:26,500 --> 00:14:27,740
most notably the automobile.

177
00:14:28,600 --> 00:14:31,220
Its popularity had a huge impact on lubricants.

178
00:14:31,900 --> 00:14:35,780
The very first automotive motor oils were crude, literally.

179
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:40,160
They were basically crude right out of the ground

180
00:14:40,161 --> 00:14:43,500
with maybe a minor amount of purification or refining.

181
00:14:44,100 --> 00:14:46,400
So there were a lot of impurities in there

182
00:14:46,401 --> 00:14:50,500
and the oxygen from the air or inside the engine

183
00:14:50,501 --> 00:14:54,080
could combine with those impurities and form sludge.

184
00:14:55,360 --> 00:14:57,540
Hot temperatures broke down the oils.

185
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:00,380
Cold temperatures made the oils too thick.

186
00:15:01,080 --> 00:15:04,900
It was very difficult for the cold oil to be pumped up out of the bottom of the engine.

187
00:15:05,500 --> 00:15:07,060
Likewise, when the engine was hot,

188
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:10,400
it was more easy for the oil to be evaporated

189
00:15:10,401 --> 00:15:12,340
and blown out the exhaust pipe as smoke.

190
00:15:12,341 --> 00:15:19,340
In short, changes in temperature trigger dramatic changes in the oil's viscosity or thickness.

191
00:15:19,341 --> 00:15:25,340
In technical terms, viscosity is the resistance to flow.

192
00:15:25,341 --> 00:15:31,340
Cold temperatures boost viscosity, while heat reduces it.

193
00:15:31,341 --> 00:15:40,340
If you heat a liquid, the molecules separate from each other and this allows the liquids to flow easier.

194
00:15:40,341 --> 00:15:44,580
If you cool a liquid, the molecules become closer to each other,

195
00:15:44,581 --> 00:15:49,380
and there is less flexibility of molecules or atoms to vibrate and move.

196
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:54,600
The lower the viscosity of a lubricant, the less cushion it provides between moving parts.

197
00:15:55,620 --> 00:15:59,200
And since heat lowers viscosity, it reduces protection against wear.

198
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:04,360
But cold temperatures can make viscosity so high that moving parts can't move,

199
00:16:04,740 --> 00:16:06,180
and a car engine won't start.

200
00:16:06,181 --> 00:16:11,140
In the 1920s, oil companies sought to solve this dilemma.

201
00:16:11,780 --> 00:16:15,740
They started making lower viscosity oils by distilling crude at lower temperatures.

202
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:20,540
Depending on the weather, motorists could alternate between thick and thin.

203
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:27,420
In the winter, they would put a much thinner oil in so that cranking would be much easier.

204
00:16:28,060 --> 00:16:31,280
And in the summer, they would have to move to a thicker oil

205
00:16:31,281 --> 00:16:36,840
so that you'd maintain that oil film thickness between the moving engine parts.

206
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:43,200
In the early 1950s, oil companies began manufacturing multigrade oils,

207
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:45,460
whose viscosity morphed with temperature.

208
00:16:47,020 --> 00:16:49,620
Now motorists could use the same oil year-round.

209
00:16:50,780 --> 00:16:53,160
Multigrade set the standard for modern motor oil.

210
00:16:55,020 --> 00:16:59,720
Multigrade motor oils act like a very thin oil at cold temperatures

211
00:16:59,721 --> 00:17:01,920
so that it's easier to start the vehicle,

212
00:17:02,440 --> 00:17:05,720
but then they open up and become more viscous at high temperatures

213
00:17:05,721 --> 00:17:08,960
so there's a thicker lubricating film to protect moving parts.

214
00:17:10,180 --> 00:17:13,180
The secret to multigrade oils is a polymer additive.

215
00:17:15,080 --> 00:17:18,940
Polymers are simple molecules that hook up to form large molecular structures.

216
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:24,180
When a multigrade oil is cold, the polymers coil up.

217
00:17:25,300 --> 00:17:29,520
But as the oil gets hot, the polymers unwind into long chains

218
00:17:29,521 --> 00:17:31,140
that stop the oil from thinning.

219
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:37,660
Additives also help turn petroleum into a wide variety of lubricants

220
00:17:37,661 --> 00:17:41,280
to lube the 2,000 different friction points in the average car.

221
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:49,080
Motor oil lubes, cleans, and cools critical parts of the engine.

222
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:52,780
The motor oil has to be very thin and free-flowing

223
00:17:52,781 --> 00:17:56,800
so that it can start in its resting place when you start the engine in the morning.

224
00:17:56,801 --> 00:18:02,620
It can be drawn up the pickup tube, go through the oil filter, it lubricates the crankshaft,

225
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:07,360
it comes up, it lubricates the camshaft, and then it trickles back down to the bottom for its next cycle.

226
00:18:09,080 --> 00:18:11,500
Automatic transmission fluid is a light oil.

227
00:18:12,640 --> 00:18:17,340
Its red color comes from a dye, added so that the fluid won't be mistaken for motor oil.

228
00:18:19,260 --> 00:18:22,720
Automatic transmission fluid is thinner in viscosity than motor oil

229
00:18:22,721 --> 00:18:28,560
because it needs to get through all these small passages and nooks and crannies within the automatic transmission.

230
00:18:29,840 --> 00:18:36,600
Additives in the fluid called friction modifiers ensure that the transmission's clutches don't slip or grab during shifting.

231
00:18:40,180 --> 00:18:42,860
Gear oil lubricates the rear differential.

232
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:49,460
It's a heavy oil with a sulfurous smell caused by an additive that helps it withstand extreme pressures.

233
00:18:50,280 --> 00:18:53,440
Gear oil differs from motor oil in that it's thicker in viscosity

234
00:18:53,441 --> 00:18:57,100
because you have all of the power from the transmission

235
00:18:57,101 --> 00:19:01,720
being carried through this pinion gear and this ring gear out to the wheels.

236
00:19:02,200 --> 00:19:05,660
So the oil needs to be thicker in viscosity to help keep those gear teeth separated.

237
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:10,620
A car's ball joints demand a lubricant that's semi-solid.

238
00:19:11,680 --> 00:19:12,380
Grease.

239
00:19:12,660 --> 00:19:16,740
Grease is a combination of a liquid lubricant

240
00:19:16,741 --> 00:19:19,660
and a thickener that kind of acts like a sponge.

241
00:19:20,260 --> 00:19:24,260
And the sponge helps hold the lubricant in place in odd locations

242
00:19:24,261 --> 00:19:30,180
where a regular liquid would typically drip out or be thrown out by high rotations.

243
00:19:31,300 --> 00:19:34,880
The continual development of new additives has made possible

244
00:19:34,881 --> 00:19:37,640
the creation of a universe of petroleum lubricants

245
00:19:37,641 --> 00:19:39,820
for a staggering range of applications.

246
00:19:42,360 --> 00:19:46,660
But today's world of lubrication isn't limited to products that come from the ground.

247
00:19:46,740 --> 00:19:50,740
Now we can make lubricants from scratch.

248
00:19:53,740 --> 00:19:58,740
In the 1850s, Samuel Keir built America's first commercial oil refinery.

249
00:19:59,740 --> 00:20:04,740
A one-barrel still in Pittsburgh that turned petroleum into kerosene.

250
00:20:05,740 --> 00:20:08,740
Lube Job will return on Modern Marvels.

251
00:20:11,740 --> 00:20:15,740
We now return to Lube Job on Modern Marvels.

252
00:20:16,740 --> 00:20:23,740
How do you keep a mammoth telescope rotating smoothly?

253
00:20:23,741 --> 00:20:33,740
That was the challenge tackled in the 1930s by engineers who designed the 200-inch Hale Telescope at Mount Palomar in California.

254
00:20:33,741 --> 00:20:39,740
The large horseshoe-shaped object we see behind us supports the north end of the telescope.

255
00:20:39,741 --> 00:20:42,740
The telescope weighs over a million pounds.

256
00:20:42,741 --> 00:20:51,300
In order for the horseshoe and its massive burden to rotate freely, the horseshoe must literally float on top of its curved support structure.

257
00:20:51,960 --> 00:20:58,060
The horseshoe is lubricated by sitting on some pads made of classic bearing material called Babbitt.

258
00:20:58,620 --> 00:21:02,100
But if it just dragged on those pads, it would have a lot of friction.

259
00:21:04,280 --> 00:21:10,080
So we inject high-pressure oil from pumps down below into pockets in these Babbitt pads.

260
00:21:10,081 --> 00:21:16,360
And when we eject it at a high enough pressure, it literally lifts the telescope up and supports it on a film of oil.

261
00:21:18,100 --> 00:21:24,280
When the pumps are on, the oil squirts continuously out of the pads at 350 pounds per square inch.

262
00:21:25,220 --> 00:21:26,880
It's then recirculating.

263
00:21:27,680 --> 00:21:33,020
Originally, it was a special oil called Mobile Flying Horse Telescope Oil No. 95,

264
00:21:33,520 --> 00:21:39,120
which was specially compounded to compensate for the seasonal temperature differences here.

265
00:21:40,080 --> 00:21:47,080
In recent years, Palomar has switched from petroleum to synthetic oil since it's less flammable.

266
00:21:50,080 --> 00:21:53,080
And synthetics also outdo petroleum lubricants on many other counts,

267
00:21:54,080 --> 00:21:59,080
a superiority that first aroused commercial interest in the early 20th century.

268
00:22:01,080 --> 00:22:08,080
In the 1920s, Standard Oil Company of Indiana developed the first commercial process

269
00:22:08,081 --> 00:22:10,747
for making synthetic hydrocarbons.

270
00:22:10,748 --> 00:22:16,080
However, due to a lack of interest as well as high production costs,

271
00:22:16,081 --> 00:22:21,080
this process was abandoned after the first pilot plant operation.

272
00:22:21,081 --> 00:22:27,080
During the same time, IG Farben company in Germany developed the same process.

273
00:22:27,081 --> 00:22:32,080
That process made an artificial hydrocarbon from coal.

274
00:22:32,081 --> 00:22:33,080
whole.

275
00:22:33,081 --> 00:22:40,080
Though other techniques can also create synthetic lubricants out of everything from petroleum

276
00:22:40,081 --> 00:22:43,940
to silica sand.

277
00:22:43,941 --> 00:22:48,900
The molecules are custom made in a lab by taking naturally occurring ingredients, breaking

278
00:22:48,901 --> 00:22:53,740
them apart, and then rebuilding them to be uniform in size, shape, and weight.

279
00:22:53,741 --> 00:22:57,860
And that's what makes synthetic oils so good is that you can take out what you don't want

280
00:22:57,861 --> 00:23:00,120
in an oil that you pump out of the ground.

281
00:23:00,121 --> 00:23:03,900
synthetic oil where you start with a pure gas for example and you make a

282
00:23:03,901 --> 00:23:08,740
polymer fluid from a couple of pure gases that you react together you know

283
00:23:08,741 --> 00:23:12,000
what you wind up with because you control the starting materials so you

284
00:23:12,001 --> 00:23:18,300
have better control of things like how viscosity changes with temperature on

285
00:23:18,301 --> 00:23:21,780
the other hand conventional lubricants made from crude oil contains some

286
00:23:21,781 --> 00:23:26,900
molecules you don't want petroleum oils are a mix of different types of

287
00:23:26,901 --> 00:23:30,260
molecules, including some that are broken or jagged.

288
00:23:30,261 --> 00:23:32,780
The early synthetic oils were developed

289
00:23:32,781 --> 00:23:34,057
to avoid this.

290
00:23:34,058 --> 00:23:41,080
The flawed molecules in petroleum easily attach to oxygen molecules, and that

291
00:23:41,081 --> 00:23:45,376
triggers oxidation, which breaks down the lubricant.

292
00:23:45,377 --> 00:23:48,020
But for Germany in the late 30s,

293
00:23:48,021 --> 00:23:50,800
The big problem with petroleum was its scarcity.

294
00:23:57,320 --> 00:24:00,000
By the time Hitler's blitzkrieg had struck Poland,

295
00:24:00,600 --> 00:24:04,860
the Germans were manufacturing synthetic lubricants for their military vehicles.

296
00:24:06,340 --> 00:24:09,660
And soon the Americans were making them too, for bombers.

297
00:24:11,640 --> 00:24:15,600
But it was the post-war development of a new aircraft, the jet,

298
00:24:15,601 --> 00:24:18,900
which mostly drove the development of synthetic lubricants.

299
00:24:20,360 --> 00:24:24,480
The first jet engines were flown during World War II by the Germans,

300
00:24:24,960 --> 00:24:28,940
and those jet engines were using whatever conventional oils were available.

301
00:24:29,460 --> 00:24:34,440
But it didn't take too long into the 1940s that we found out that conventional petroleum oils

302
00:24:34,441 --> 00:24:38,060
just didn't have the thermal stability to be able to withstand the high temperatures

303
00:24:38,061 --> 00:24:40,160
without breaking down in jet engines.

304
00:24:40,161 --> 00:24:45,400
High temperatures in the engine can oxidize the oil and thin it down.

305
00:24:46,200 --> 00:24:50,100
Synthetic lubricants can handle that much better than hydrocarbon oils can.

306
00:24:51,520 --> 00:24:55,360
Jet aircraft also fly at higher altitudes than piston planes.

307
00:24:56,500 --> 00:25:01,480
There, atmospheric pressure is lower, which causes petroleum lubricants to evaporate.

308
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:06,040
Hydrocarbon lubricants have smaller molecules as part of the mixture,

309
00:25:06,041 --> 00:25:10,040
and these smaller molecules will evaporate away or boil away.

310
00:25:10,160 --> 00:25:14,240
creating a highly flammable vapor.

311
00:25:16,400 --> 00:25:19,320
However, it was the cold weather benefits of synthetics

312
00:25:19,321 --> 00:25:23,380
that inspired one of the visionaries who brought these lubricants to motorists.

313
00:25:27,220 --> 00:25:32,780
In the mid-1960s, Lieutenant Colonel Albert Amatuzio, a jet fighter pilot,

314
00:25:33,220 --> 00:25:36,440
began experimenting with used synthetic turbine oil from his jet.

315
00:25:36,441 --> 00:25:42,500
He was flying in the northern climates where it's quite cold and knew that it worked in his airplanes.

316
00:25:42,940 --> 00:25:47,900
He decided to experiment with his cars and see if it would help them start in the cold winters of Minnesota.

317
00:25:49,480 --> 00:25:53,500
It did help, so he launched a business to exploit his research.

318
00:25:54,520 --> 00:25:58,720
And working with several chemical companies, he developed a history-making motor oil.

319
00:25:58,721 --> 00:26:08,520
In 1972, it became the first 100% synthetic car motor oil to be approved by the American Petroleum Institute.

320
00:26:11,660 --> 00:26:17,700
Synthetic motor oils excel in winter cold because they aren't hampered by harmful chemicals found in petroleum.

321
00:26:20,300 --> 00:26:26,040
There's just a lot of paraffin-type molecules that make wax that occurs in nature,

322
00:26:26,041 --> 00:26:28,840
and it finds its way into crude oil.

323
00:26:30,180 --> 00:26:34,480
At cold temperatures, the paraffin hardens, hindering the flow of motor oil.

324
00:26:35,520 --> 00:26:40,660
From a cold start in wintertime, it can take as long as 20 minutes

325
00:26:40,661 --> 00:26:43,860
for the oil to get to the working parts in the engine.

326
00:26:44,460 --> 00:26:47,500
In 20 minutes, you can have disastrous wear occur.

327
00:26:49,840 --> 00:26:53,660
Today, all of the major oil companies manufacture synthetic motor oils.

328
00:26:53,661 --> 00:26:58,220
Though they cost two to four times as much as petroleum products,

329
00:26:58,800 --> 00:27:02,340
synthetics now rate as one of the fastest-growing segments of the oil industry.

330
00:27:04,980 --> 00:27:08,080
But synthetic lubricants can also be solids.

331
00:27:09,200 --> 00:27:14,600
The world's best-known solid lubricant, Teflon, was discovered by accident in 1938.

332
00:27:16,280 --> 00:27:18,880
Dr. Roy Plunkett was a chemist for DuPont,

333
00:27:18,881 --> 00:27:23,200
and he was investigating the manufacture of new refrigerants.

334
00:27:23,660 --> 00:27:25,900
for freon refrigeration.

335
00:27:25,901 --> 00:27:28,740
Plunkett and his assistant had stored a refrigerant gas

336
00:27:28,741 --> 00:27:32,400
in a pressurized cylinder kept in dry ice.

337
00:27:32,401 --> 00:27:35,500
But when they later opened it, no gas came out

338
00:27:35,501 --> 00:27:36,980
because the pressure and temperature

339
00:27:36,981 --> 00:27:40,780
had caused the gas molecules to form long solid strands,

340
00:27:40,781 --> 00:27:42,440
polymers.

341
00:27:42,441 --> 00:27:44,680
And when the cylinder was turned upside down,

342
00:27:44,681 --> 00:27:46,380
lo and behold, white powder came out,

343
00:27:46,381 --> 00:27:49,920
which was a surprise because tetrofluorethylene gas

344
00:27:49,921 --> 00:27:52,660
had been put into the cylinder and a fluoropolymer

345
00:27:52,661 --> 00:27:54,460
had come out of the cylinder.

346
00:27:54,461 --> 00:27:59,040
And furthermore, the cylinder was then taken and cut in half.

347
00:27:59,041 --> 00:28:01,460
There was white powder all on the inside.

348
00:28:01,461 --> 00:28:06,540
And this indeed was the first sample of Teflon fluoropolymer.

349
00:28:06,541 --> 00:28:10,100
Plunkett discovered that Teflon stood up to corrosive chemicals.

350
00:28:10,101 --> 00:28:12,440
That's why one of its initial uses was on the project

351
00:28:12,441 --> 00:28:15,260
to build the first atomic bomb.

352
00:28:15,261 --> 00:28:17,940
Gaskets made out of Teflon fluoropolymer

353
00:28:17,941 --> 00:28:20,140
were very good in corrosive environments.

354
00:28:20,141 --> 00:28:26,820
and the Manhattan Project required corrosive chemistry to purify and enrich the uranium.

355
00:28:27,780 --> 00:28:30,240
But Teflon had another useful trait.

356
00:28:30,940 --> 00:28:35,640
It was slippery, more so than any natural solid lubricant like graphite.

357
00:28:36,320 --> 00:28:40,260
And that slickness soon turned Teflon into a household word.

358
00:28:41,420 --> 00:28:46,520
The secret to Teflon's slipperiness is an atom which comes from a mineral fluorospar.

359
00:28:46,521 --> 00:28:52,760
What makes Teflon fluoropolymer so slippery is the fact that the fluorine atom is an integral

360
00:28:52,761 --> 00:28:55,200
part of the polymer in the chemistry.

361
00:28:55,201 --> 00:29:00,800
The outside surface is shielded by the fluorine atom, which is not reactive with most chemicals

362
00:29:00,801 --> 00:29:05,360
and things do not want to stick and slide off the surface.

363
00:29:05,361 --> 00:29:08,800
Lubricating challenges at home can't compare with those in the heavens.

364
00:29:08,801 --> 00:29:13,520
The environment of space may be hostile, but lubricants keep our craft running smoothly.

365
00:29:16,520 --> 00:29:21,160
The first synthetic hydrocarbons, ancestors of today's synthetic motor oils,

366
00:29:21,161 --> 00:29:26,480
were created by the chemists Charles Friedel and James Crafts in 1877.

367
00:29:28,040 --> 00:29:32,420
Lube Job will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

368
00:29:36,500 --> 00:29:40,320
We now return to Lube Job on Modern Marvels.

369
00:29:40,321 --> 00:29:48,500
It's no exaggeration to say that the exploration of the heavens depends on space-age lubrication,

370
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:53,780
because the harsh new realm of the final frontier has placed new demands on lubricants.

371
00:29:55,080 --> 00:29:57,060
First of all, they had to do with vacuum.

372
00:29:57,240 --> 00:30:01,980
There's a very hard vacuum in space, and we had to keep the lubricants from evaporating off quickly.

373
00:30:02,560 --> 00:30:06,940
Second of all, the temperature extremes in space are much more severe than Earth.

374
00:30:06,941 --> 00:30:12,620
There's radiation, there's dust and debris if you're on the moon or Mars,

375
00:30:13,200 --> 00:30:16,880
and in addition, it's an extremely long way to go to change your oil.

376
00:30:19,040 --> 00:30:23,140
Plus, lubricants had to resist fire, a danger posed by rockets.

377
00:30:24,280 --> 00:30:29,920
That was one of the main reasons back in the 60s that NASA adopted a new family of lubricants.

378
00:30:29,921 --> 00:30:39,720
NASA was first attracted to Krytox because it was non-flammable and wouldn't react with the oxygen and fuels that they used in their engines.

379
00:30:41,540 --> 00:30:51,100
Krytox is DuPont's brand name for a class of lubricants called perfluoropolyethers, or PFPEs, which the company invented in 1959.

380
00:30:51,101 --> 00:30:59,980
They're synthesized strings of molecules that contain the same slippery fluorine atoms as

381
00:30:59,981 --> 00:31:05,667
Teflon. PFPEs can be turned into either an oil or grease.

382
00:31:05,668 --> 00:31:08,560
The grease on the left is the

383
00:31:08,561 --> 00:31:14,703
Krytox. The grease on the right is a hydrocarbon lubricant.

384
00:31:14,704 --> 00:31:18,660
As you can see, the Krytox is unburned

385
00:31:18,661 --> 00:31:23,300
and still looking good the hydrocarbon lubricant is still burning and will continue to burn until

386
00:31:23,301 --> 00:31:29,540
it consumes itself and pfpe's had other traits that made them perfect for space

387
00:31:30,740 --> 00:31:36,100
they were slow to evaporate resistant to radiation and performed an extreme cold

388
00:31:39,220 --> 00:31:43,860
at low temperatures krytox dramatically outperforms hydrocarbon lubricants

389
00:31:43,861 --> 00:31:48,860
Both samples have been chilled to minus 94 Fahrenheit.

390
00:31:48,861 --> 00:31:51,860
The Krytox still pours.

391
00:31:55,860 --> 00:31:58,860
The hydrocarbon is frozen solid.

392
00:32:00,860 --> 00:32:05,860
NASA has employed PFPEs on everything from spacesuits to the Mars rovers.

393
00:32:05,861 --> 00:32:09,860
PFPEs were the only choice for the deep freeze of Mars.

394
00:32:09,861 --> 00:32:13,320
Mars tends to be kind of a cold place.

395
00:32:13,900 --> 00:32:17,740
And one of the things that's nice about the fluorocarbon oils

396
00:32:17,741 --> 00:32:20,600
is that we can really tailor their molecular weights

397
00:32:20,601 --> 00:32:25,080
and come up with an oil that flows very freely at very, very low temperatures.

398
00:32:25,240 --> 00:32:30,080
At temperatures hundreds of degrees below zero, we still get good flowability.

399
00:32:32,620 --> 00:32:36,940
PFPEs have lubricated the Mars rovers since the very first mission in 1997.

400
00:32:36,941 --> 00:32:42,940
Every mechanism that keeps a rover roving depends on them.

401
00:32:42,941 --> 00:32:46,940
There's the six wheels and four of those six wheels steer.

402
00:32:46,941 --> 00:32:51,940
There's a suspension that holds onto the wheels that also articulates to keep all six wheels on the surface.

403
00:32:51,941 --> 00:32:54,940
Over here we have the high gain antenna.

404
00:32:54,941 --> 00:32:58,940
This is the two axis gimbal, again lubricated with PFPEs.

405
00:32:58,941 --> 00:33:01,940
And this is what we communicate to Earth with.

406
00:33:01,941 --> 00:33:12,940
Also lubed by PFPEs, the rover's mechanical arm and its mast, which aims cameras.

407
00:33:12,941 --> 00:33:20,940
But PFPEs do have one drawback, which surfaces in zero-gravity environments.

408
00:33:20,941 --> 00:33:25,940
Like most things that aren't nailed down, they want to go places.

409
00:33:25,941 --> 00:33:30,940
One of the big challenges of space lubrication is that we're dealing with a zero-g or vacuum environment.

410
00:33:30,941 --> 00:33:35,360
In that kind of situation, the lubricant will have a tendency to want to migrate away from

411
00:33:35,361 --> 00:33:40,280
the surface to which it was applied, which is called creep.

412
00:33:40,281 --> 00:33:44,620
NASA thinks it may have been a creeping lubricant which caused controlled gyroscopes on the

413
00:33:44,621 --> 00:33:51,280
International Space Station to fail.

414
00:33:51,281 --> 00:33:54,720
Pennzoil Company solved the creep issue in the 1980s.

415
00:33:54,721 --> 00:33:59,220
They found out they could use a leftover material from their refineries to make a new lubricant

416
00:33:59,221 --> 00:34:01,800
called penzane.

417
00:34:01,801 --> 00:34:04,020
Penzane is built starting with cyclopentadiene,

418
00:34:04,021 --> 00:34:07,100
which is a byproduct of gasoline out of the refinery.

419
00:34:07,101 --> 00:34:08,880
It's then reacted with Gorbet alcohols

420
00:34:08,881 --> 00:34:12,000
to make the finished product.

421
00:34:12,001 --> 00:34:14,880
Besides working as an oil, this synthetic hydrocarbon

422
00:34:14,881 --> 00:34:20,980
can be turned into a grease, which in zero gravity stays put.

423
00:34:20,981 --> 00:34:22,840
Penzane is particularly resistant to creep

424
00:34:22,841 --> 00:34:24,620
because it has a high surface tension.

425
00:34:24,621 --> 00:34:27,360
So it adheres very well to the surface to which it's applied,

426
00:34:27,361 --> 00:34:29,100
and it stays there.

427
00:34:29,101 --> 00:34:32,900
PFPEs used by NASA have been spun off into many consumer products,

428
00:34:33,100 --> 00:34:36,220
from computer hard disks to gear for slippery slopes.

429
00:34:37,060 --> 00:34:40,180
In working with the Krytox PFPE products,

430
00:34:40,181 --> 00:34:45,800
we learned that it worked amazingly well on skis and snowboards.

431
00:34:50,680 --> 00:34:53,660
Another aerospace lubricant took off with consumers

432
00:34:53,661 --> 00:34:56,680
to become the world's most famous household lubricant.

433
00:34:56,681 --> 00:35:01,920
It was invented by a tiny San Diego firm called the Rocket Chemical Company.

434
00:35:03,120 --> 00:35:07,320
In 1953, they were hired to fix a problem with a rocket.

435
00:35:11,800 --> 00:35:16,540
There was a problem with condensation and corrosion with the Atlas Space Rocket.

436
00:35:16,740 --> 00:35:22,620
And from these original vials back in the 50s, the formulation of WD-40 was created.

437
00:35:22,621 --> 00:35:30,220
The formula which was developed by chemists here after 39 tries, we ended up with the 40th formula.

438
00:35:31,140 --> 00:35:33,040
WD stands for water displacement.

439
00:35:33,280 --> 00:35:36,960
40th formula was the magnificent formulation that solved that problem.

440
00:35:37,520 --> 00:35:42,860
And from that came the first aerosol can of WD-40 that was manufactured.

441
00:35:44,200 --> 00:35:51,920
The liquid worked not only as a rust inhibitor, but also as a solvent, penetrant, cleaning agents, and lubricant.

442
00:35:52,620 --> 00:35:57,300
Workers at the company that made the Atlas rocket took notice.

443
00:35:59,060 --> 00:36:03,440
The engineers and the plant workers found many uses for the product

444
00:36:03,441 --> 00:36:09,080
and really identified that those uses were things that they wanted to use WD-40 on at home.

445
00:36:09,400 --> 00:36:12,820
So they would sneak it home and use it and tell their friends about it,

446
00:36:12,821 --> 00:36:17,140
and that's the way the product WD-40 got some of its early usage.

447
00:36:18,680 --> 00:36:21,680
When the makers of WD-40 saw what was going on,

448
00:36:21,681 --> 00:36:24,560
They decided to cash in and sell it to the public.

449
00:36:26,380 --> 00:36:30,740
The product, in fact, was one of the first products to be aerosolized.

450
00:36:30,980 --> 00:36:34,980
That packaging change made it easy for the consumer to use the brand.

451
00:36:37,160 --> 00:36:39,780
In case you hadn't noticed, the marketing worked.

452
00:36:40,680 --> 00:36:45,080
The WD-40 can in the United States is in 8 out of 10 households.

453
00:36:45,400 --> 00:36:49,840
In fact, it's probably in more households in the U.S. than Coca-Cola.

454
00:36:49,841 --> 00:36:53,940
and then from there it's in 160 countries around the world.

455
00:36:55,200 --> 00:36:59,300
WD-40 is mixed by what the company affectionately calls brewmasters.

456
00:37:00,320 --> 00:37:04,840
Ken East serves as the lone American brewmaster at the company's San Diego headquarters.

457
00:37:05,200 --> 00:37:10,240
WD-40 consists of six raw materials that I bring in through our valving system.

458
00:37:10,740 --> 00:37:15,180
I bring them in, they come in through here, I monitor each raw material.

459
00:37:15,181 --> 00:37:18,440
We get so many gallons, we take it into the system.

460
00:37:18,441 --> 00:37:22,140
Once it goes in here, we bring it into the vat.

461
00:37:24,660 --> 00:37:28,000
It takes 30 minutes of stirring to brew the final cocktail.

462
00:37:29,280 --> 00:37:32,760
The recipe is top secret, known to just a handful of employees.

463
00:37:33,440 --> 00:37:35,360
So, what are those secret ingredients?

464
00:37:35,580 --> 00:37:36,520
Oh, I can't tell you that.

465
00:37:37,100 --> 00:37:38,480
I'd have to kill you if I told you that.

466
00:37:42,740 --> 00:37:45,540
Over the years, all anyone else has been able to learn

467
00:37:45,541 --> 00:37:52,020
is that WD-40 is a blend of petroleum distillates, a hydrocarbon solvent, and a petroleum base oil.

468
00:37:54,980 --> 00:37:59,891
The formula has remained secret and it's locked in a bank vault.

469
00:37:59,892 --> 00:38:02,500
I don't know the formula of WD-40,

470
00:38:03,700 --> 00:38:14,020
but in it are lubricants and fragrances and anti-corrosives that all come together to make

471
00:38:14,021 --> 00:38:16,460
a formula that does many many things.

472
00:38:18,460 --> 00:38:21,460
But there are things that today's lubricants can't do

473
00:38:21,461 --> 00:38:23,380
when it comes to the machines of tomorrow.

474
00:38:24,420 --> 00:38:28,140
Micro devices and the next jet engines are creating sticky problems

475
00:38:28,141 --> 00:38:30,820
that demand new lubricating solutions.

476
00:38:34,540 --> 00:38:39,900
In 1992, Denver firefighters dislodged a naked burglar

477
00:38:39,901 --> 00:38:43,080
stuck in a 14-inch diameter air vent

478
00:38:43,081 --> 00:38:45,680
by spraying him with WD-40.

479
00:38:46,920 --> 00:38:49,520
Lubejob will return on Modern Marvels

480
00:38:49,521 --> 00:38:51,540
here on the History Channel.

481
00:38:55,380 --> 00:38:59,040
We now return to Lubejob on Modern Marvels.

482
00:39:02,140 --> 00:39:06,780
With technology pushing machines to new size and speed extremes,

483
00:39:07,400 --> 00:39:10,660
engineers are scrambling to expand friction-reducing possibilities.

484
00:39:13,080 --> 00:39:16,200
and the hurdles get bigger as machines get smaller.

485
00:39:17,720 --> 00:39:20,680
Microelectromechanical systems, or MEMS,

486
00:39:21,000 --> 00:39:23,660
are being developed for sensors in automobile tires,

487
00:39:24,640 --> 00:39:28,000
anti-lock braking systems, and four-wheel drive transmissions.

488
00:39:29,020 --> 00:39:33,920
The problem with lubing MEMS is that the laws of nature don't work the same on a micro scale.

489
00:39:35,900 --> 00:39:39,000
Well, the main challenges associated with lubricating micromachines

490
00:39:39,001 --> 00:39:40,780
come from the fact that they're so small,

491
00:39:41,360 --> 00:39:42,760
and they have very little inertia.

492
00:39:42,761 --> 00:39:45,960
So these devices are typically designed to operate quickly.

493
00:39:46,360 --> 00:39:50,340
Lubricating oils are just not appropriate because the device would be sitting in a bath of fluid

494
00:39:50,341 --> 00:39:52,520
and would have to shear that fluid every time it moves.

495
00:39:55,220 --> 00:39:59,200
Researchers at Sandia National Labs are tackling this issue

496
00:39:59,201 --> 00:40:02,920
in the Microsystems and Engineering Sciences Applications Project.

497
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:07,420
The biggest challenge of using any lubricant for microsystems

498
00:40:07,421 --> 00:40:10,260
is getting the lubricant to the sliding surfaces where it's needed.

499
00:40:10,900 --> 00:40:13,120
Frequently, these devices have very complex shape,

500
00:40:13,440 --> 00:40:17,300
and the sliding surfaces are deeply buried inside the mechanism.

501
00:40:18,440 --> 00:40:23,700
The researchers have found one solution in so-called gas-deposited lubricating films.

502
00:40:25,860 --> 00:40:30,280
One of the chief ways of lubricating microsystems is with gas-deposited films.

503
00:40:30,820 --> 00:40:33,980
And the way that works is the molecules are evaporated from some source,

504
00:40:33,981 --> 00:40:37,000
they move through the air, and then land on the surfaces,

505
00:40:37,440 --> 00:40:39,800
and they can migrate along those surfaces to where they're needed.

506
00:40:40,260 --> 00:40:47,460
The molecules of the lubricant and alcohol can easily negotiate the narrow gaps in this micro machine,

507
00:40:47,860 --> 00:40:50,020
which measures only 2 millimeters across,

508
00:40:50,840 --> 00:40:53,400
about the width of the lead in a typical wooden pencil,

509
00:40:54,060 --> 00:40:58,760
and about 15 microns thick, just one-fourth the diameter of a human hair.

510
00:41:00,500 --> 00:41:02,820
The machine is being used to measure friction.

511
00:41:03,180 --> 00:41:05,260
The lubricant is everywhere on this device,

512
00:41:05,660 --> 00:41:08,080
but contact only takes place between this movable beam

513
00:41:08,081 --> 00:41:11,200
and one of these posts that are anchored to the substrate.

514
00:41:13,720 --> 00:41:15,180
After coating the device,

515
00:41:15,560 --> 00:41:18,740
the lubricant's molecules chemically attach to the machine's surfaces.

516
00:41:20,500 --> 00:41:24,180
The molecules of this film can be engineered to have one end that is sticky

517
00:41:24,181 --> 00:41:26,840
so that it reacts with the silicon surface and will attach,

518
00:41:27,200 --> 00:41:30,560
and the other end that is very slippery so that things slide over it very easily.

519
00:41:35,200 --> 00:41:37,760
For machines you don't need a microscope to see,

520
00:41:38,080 --> 00:41:40,740
The answer to lubrication may be found in the air.

521
00:41:41,400 --> 00:41:46,480
This is a microturbine power generating system manufactured by Capstone Turbine Corporation.

522
00:41:47,400 --> 00:41:52,420
The heart of the machine is the generator that runs at 96,000 revolutions per minute.

523
00:41:53,420 --> 00:41:58,040
What I have in front of me is a cross-section of a Capstone microturbine generator.

524
00:41:58,840 --> 00:42:04,020
What happens during the combustion process is we bring air through the turbine,

525
00:42:04,021 --> 00:42:07,480
which drives a shaft and compressor wheel

526
00:42:07,481 --> 00:42:11,420
and drives a generator which produces 60 kilowatts of energy.

527
00:42:11,421 --> 00:42:14,880
What I have in front of me is that shaft removed

528
00:42:14,881 --> 00:42:19,100
which rides here where my fingers are on cushions of air

529
00:42:19,101 --> 00:42:23,380
in the single moving part of a capstone microturbine.

530
00:42:24,920 --> 00:42:29,200
The same technology will one day revolutionize jet aircraft engines,

531
00:42:29,680 --> 00:42:30,940
making them oil-free.

532
00:42:30,941 --> 00:42:36,620
That's the dream behind a NASA research project that created this test rig.

533
00:42:38,680 --> 00:42:43,860
Like the power generating systems, it floats a spinning turbine shaft on a cushion of air.

534
00:42:45,740 --> 00:42:49,880
The advantages of air over conventional lubricants is less drag.

535
00:42:50,440 --> 00:42:52,340
It doesn't break down over time.

536
00:42:52,940 --> 00:42:57,260
It's not needed to be replenished as conventional oils or greases do.

537
00:42:57,261 --> 00:43:02,500
and it increases the overall performance and reliability of the rotating parts.

538
00:43:04,360 --> 00:43:09,300
This machine's secret is a foil air bearing, which has two components.

539
00:43:09,840 --> 00:43:15,280
The top foil, made of a high-temperature alloy, and a corrugated bump foil.

540
00:43:17,360 --> 00:43:20,400
At speeds above 2,000 revolutions per minute,

541
00:43:20,840 --> 00:43:24,300
a thin film of air separates the shaft from the bearing surfaces.

542
00:43:24,301 --> 00:43:36,150
If you provide this kind of an air bearing for jet engines, it turns out that you can not only eliminate 15% of the engine weight, but you can also eliminate half of

543
00:43:36,151 --> 00:43:38,520
all the engine maintenance costs.

544
00:43:41,920 --> 00:43:44,560
Air lubrication is also clean.

545
00:43:45,760 --> 00:43:50,400
This futuristic technology doesn't leak or burn oil like conventional turbo machinery.

546
00:43:51,520 --> 00:43:53,960
And there's no used oil to get rid of.

547
00:43:54,300 --> 00:44:00,314
It's abundant as a resource. It's green friendly. It's environmentally friendly.

548
00:44:00,315 --> 00:44:11,140
And more importantly, it provides for improvements in product performance heretofore not known in conventional types of bearings and lubricants.

549
00:44:11,141 --> 00:44:18,280
With air and the invention of new lubricants, our machines will be able to go further and

550
00:44:18,281 --> 00:44:22,380
faster for longer periods of time.

551
00:44:22,381 --> 00:44:25,140
That's been the purpose of lubricants from the start.

552
00:44:25,141 --> 00:44:33,900
From helping a chariot axle spin to allowing robotic arms on Mars rovers to move.

553
00:44:33,901 --> 00:44:38,260
Lubricants are crucial to the advance of technology.

554
00:44:38,261 --> 00:44:41,560
They literally keep the wheels of progress turning.

